==English==

===Etymology===
Late 16th century, from earlier {{m|en|heyda}} (1520s), as exclamation – compare {{m|en|hey}}, {{m|enm|hei}}. Sense “period of success, vigor” is a respelling as '''heyday''' based on unrelated {{m|en|day}} (as “period of time”) – compare {{m|en|day in the sun}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈheɪdeɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-heyday.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪdeɪ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[period]] of [[success]], [[popularity]], or [[power]]; [[prime]].
#: {{ux|en|The early twentieth century was the '''heyday''' of the steam locomotive.}}
#* {{RQ:John Ford Whore|page=57|passage=Must your hot ytch and plurisie of lust, The '''heyday''' of your luxury, be fedd Up to a surfeite, and could none but I Be pickt out to be cloake to your close tricks, Your belly-sports?}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} An [[exultation]] of the [[spirit]]s; [[gaiety]]; [[frolic]].

